# Gravitation: From Falling Apples to Orbiting Satellites

> The same force that drops an apple steers the Moon. This lesson
> runs from Kepler's orbits through Newton's universal law, g, escape
> velocity, satellites, and tides.

## 1. Kepler's Laws of Planetary Motion

Tycho Brahe's naked-eye data let Johannes Kepler abandon perfect
circles for **ellipses** — decades before Newton explained why.

| Law | Statement | Consequence |
| --- | --- | --- |
| First (orbit) | planets move in ellipses with the Sun at one focus | no circle is needed |
| Second (areas) | the Sun–planet line sweeps equal areas in equal times | planets speed up near the Sun |
| Third (periods) | T² ∝ a³ for every planet | distant planets crawl |

[info title="T squared over a cubed"]
For Earth, T = 1 year with a = 1 AU; for Mars, a = 1.52 AU, so
T = √(1.52³) ≈ 1.88 years — as observed.
[/info]

## 2. Newton's Universal Law of Gravitation

Every particle attracts every other with a force along the line
joining them.

@math label="eq:gravitation"
F = \frac{G m_{1} m_{2}}{r^{2}}
@/math

G = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N·m²/kg² is the **universal gravitational
constant** — the same everywhere in the universe.

[example]
Two 100 kg students sitting 1 m apart pull at each other with
F = 6.67 × 10⁻⁷ N — less than the weight of a grain of rice.
Gravity between everyday objects is feeble; only planet-sized
masses make it rule the sky.
[/example]

[note title="Weighing the Earth"]
In 1798 Henry Cavendish measured G in the laboratory with a
twisting torsion balance. Plugging G into g = GM/R² gave Earth's
mass for the first time — about 6 × 10²⁴ kg.
[/note]

## 3. Acceleration Due to Gravity

@math label="eq:gravity"
g = \frac{GM}{R^{2}} \approx 9.8\ \mathrm{m/s^{2}}
@/math

Every body near Earth's surface falls with the same g, whatever
its mass. The value falls slightly with **height** (9.8 → 8.7 m/s²
at the ISS's 400 km) and also decreases with **depth** toward the
centre. At the equator g ≈ 9.78 m/s², at the poles ≈ 9.83 m/s².

Astronauts float in orbit not because gravity vanishes but
because the whole station falls together with them — apparent
**weightlessness** in free fall.

## 4. Mass versus Weight

| Aspect | Mass | Weight |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Meaning | amount of matter | gravitational force on it |
| Formula | — | W = mg |
| Unit | kilogram (kg) | newton (N) |
| Scalar or vector | scalar | vector (downward) |
| Varies with location? | never | yes — with g |
| Measured by | beam balance | spring balance |

[example]
A 60 kg student has weight W = 60 × 9.8 ≈ 588 N on Earth but
only about 97 N on the Moon (g ≈ 1.6 m/s²); the mass stays 60 kg
everywhere.
[/example]

## 5. Escape Velocity and Orbital Velocity

**Escape velocity** is the launch speed that lets a body leave a
planet's gravity forever.

@math label="eq:escape"
v_{e} = \sqrt{2gR} = 11.2\ \mathrm{km/s\ (Earth)}
@/math

**Orbital velocity** is smaller: a satellite need only balance
gravity as a centripetal force, giving about 7.9 km/s just above
Earth's surface. The Moon, farther out, needs only about 1 km/s.

## 6. Artificial Satellites

A satellite is a projectile moving fast enough sideways that it
keeps missing the Earth — Newton's own thought experiment.

| Feature | Geostationary | Polar (sun-synchronous) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Period | 24 h (matches Earth's spin) | about 100 min |
| Height | ~36,000 km | 700–800 km |
| Path | west → east above the equator | pole to pole |
| Appears | fixed in the sky | crosses every latitude |
| Uses | DTH TV, weather, communication | mapping, spying, crop and forest monitoring |

@diagram tree id="satellite-family" layout="tree" aria="Artificial satellites classified by orbit type"
  "Artificial satellite" -> "Geostationary: TV, weather, phones"
  "Artificial satellite" -> "Polar: mapping, crops, monitoring"
  "Artificial satellite" -> "Navigation: GPS-style positioning"
@/diagram

## 7. Tides: Gravity on the Water

Ocean **tides** peak where the Moon's pull is strongest; a weaker
solar pull adds to it. At new and full moon the two line up to give
**spring tides**; at half moon they partially cancel, giving gentler
**neap tides**. India's tallest tidal range is in the Gulf of
Khambhat.

## 8. Quick Revision

| Item | Remember |
| --- | --- |
| Kepler's third law | T² ∝ a³ |
| Universal law | F = Gm₁m₂/r², G = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N·m²/kg² |
| Surface gravity | g = GM/R² ≈ 9.8 m/s² |
| Mass vs weight | kg never changes; W = mg varies with g |
| Escape velocity (Earth) | 11.2 km/s |
| Geostationary | 24 h, ~36,000 km, over the equator |
| Strong tides | spring tides at new and full moon |
